ions' own logic or the signal-handling control flow):\n\n1. Read the surrounding function to understand what the catch is actually guarding against and what happens today when it swallows silently (what does the function return/do next).\n2. Decide, from the real code, whether this catch is:\n   (a) An EXPECTED/benign condition - the process-already-gone case described above for the six kill sites, or a best-effort marker/config read/write for the other two. For these, keep the swallow (do not change control flow or add noisy logging for the expected path), but narrow to the specific expected error code (ESRCH for kill calls, ENOENT where a marker/config file may not exist) and add minimal visibility (see point 3) for anything else unexpected - same return value, same behavior, just visible.\n   (b) A genuinely UNEXPECTED-error catch with no narrower expected case. Add minimal visibility only: a one-line log. This file has no existing logger/journal convention of its own - use `console.error('[tools/concurrency-guard.mjs] <short context naming the function/operation>:', error?.message ?? error)`. Do NOT add a throw, do NOT change the function's return value or control flow, do NOT change what happens next.\n3. If a catch binds no error variable, add a REAL binding and actually reference it in a console.error call - do not just rename-and-ignore a binding (including via a `void error;` no-op).\n\nDo not refactor anything beyond the flagged catch blocks. Do not reformat unrelated code. Do not rename functions or change exports. Preserve every existing behavior path exactly except for the narrowing/visibility described above.\n\nYour workspace does not include the test suite, so you cannot run it yourself - the orchestrator runs the real full suite against your output after integration, and will also get this reviewed by Opus before merge given the sensitivity (process-kill), and will send back any issue as a follow-up. List, in your final summary, which of the 8 flagged lines you touched, case (a) vs (b), with one line each, and confirm the kill()/apply()/restore() calls themselves are unchanged.",
